  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nOur goal is to find a step function h:[0,2]\u2192[0,1] with \u222bh=1 that minimizes the maximum overlap integral C5 = max\u2096\u222bh(x)(1\u2212h(x+k))dx. \nThe previous program uses temperature annealing with SLSQP optimization, but we can improve by:\n1. Utilizing a more efficient optimization method (Adam-like optimizer) with automatic annealing\n2. Implementing a multi-resolution strategy that progressively increases points from 280 to 560 and beyond\n3. Introducing periodic support to better capture shift-invariant behavior\n4. Using smarter repair and constraint handling\n\nThis approach balances exploration vs exploitation and allows us to escape local optima while maintaining the guarantee of feasible candidates.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe represent h as an array of real numbers between 0 and 1 with fixed sum (n/2 exactly). The objective is to minimize:\n  C5 = (max_k \u222bh(x)(1\u2212h(x+k))dx) * (2/n)\n\nConstraints:\n  0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 \u2200i\n  \u2211h_i = n/2 (exactly)\n\nWe'll use a transformed objective based on the sum of squares to improve optimization performance:\n  f(h) = (1/T) * log(\u2211exp(T*(maxCorr(original) - corr)))\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Parent solution handling:\n   - Take parent solution at (200, 300) points if available\n   - For each new resolution level (from 280 to 560), interpolate parent with bounds preservation\n\n2. Optimization:\n   - Use temperature-annealed Adam optimizer with:\n        Objective: f(h) = -(max_correlation_fourier)\n        Steps:\n          \u2022 Forward pass: compute four exponential smoothing runs to get max correlation\n          \u2022 Grad: compute via perturbation (strip gradients)\n          \u2022 Optimizer: Adam with momentum, adaptive learning rates and constraints handling\n\n3. Repair and feasibility:\n   - Repair function: linear interpolation + gradient descent projection to satisfy sum constraint\n   - Feasibility check: clamp to [0,1] with careful balance between constraints\n\n4. Levels strategy:\n   Level 1: 280\u2192300 points with detailed optimization\n   Level 2: 300\u2192350 points with medium intensity\n   Level 3: 350+ fine points with smart exploration\n\n5. Search choices:\n   - Each level's history is stored to track top candidates\n   - Random restart during optimization with 5 patterns\n   - Progressively more aggressive exploration until doubling of points\n\nCompute budget:\n   Estimate: Each level~60-90 seconds; up to 13 solutions\n   Check elapsed time before moving to next level/simplification\n\nValidation and return contract:\n   For every solution: check bounds and constraints.\n   Keep best solution regardless (for n=280 at least)\n   Return highest resolution candidate (or best run-ending)\n\nLet's proceed with implementation.",
